WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.570
So zooming out a little bit from the player related stuff,

2
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.049
there's a set of classes that we should talk about that are

3
00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:10.210
regarded under what is strictly called the Unreal gameplay

4
00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.089
framework that apply more to the world than a specific player

5
00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:16.129
that we should just talk about a little bit.

6
00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:18.289
So those are the game mode, the game state,

7
00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:19.649
and the game instance.

8
00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:23.129
Starting with the game mode, as the name might imply,

9
00:00:23.129 --> 00:00:25.609
this is kind of the rules of the game,

10
00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:28.170
the kind of more static data about the game.

11
00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.210
There might be things like, how many players are there?

12
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.850
How many players are allowed to join this game?

13
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.250
Where might those players spawn?

14
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:40.450
How many points do you need to win the game?

15
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.369
And quite importantly, for both online and single player

16
00:00:44.369 --> 00:00:47.890
games, the game mode also specifies the default classes

17
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.130
that we're going to spawn for each of these things.

18
00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.490
So it specifies your default player classes.

19
00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.689
It specifies the player control class

20
00:00:54.689 --> 00:00:57.229
to spawn for each player.

21
00:00:57.229 --> 00:00:59.590
Moving on from that, we have the game state.

22
00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:03.950
If you think about this as the real time state of the game,

23
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:07.109
in the context of a networked game,

24
00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:09.150
the game mode is going to be server only.

25
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.750
It's going to be authoritative.

26
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.390
But the game state is actually a replicated actor.

27
00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.750
So the game state is the shared real time

28
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.790
state of that game between both the server and the client.

29
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.989
So if we took capture the flag game as an example,

30
00:01:24.989 --> 00:01:27.390
the game mode would specify maybe how many points

31
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.469
you need to score.

32
00:01:28.469 --> 00:01:30.670
But the actual current score would be something

33
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.230
we store in the game state.

34
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.390
So that can be replicated across all the clients

35
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.230
so everyone has an up to date idea of what

36
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.599
the current score is.

37
00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:40.840
The third class that we need to talk about is the game

38
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:41.560
instance.

39
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.280
And this is for storing our persistent data

40
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.680
throughout an entire gameplay session.

41
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.560
Whilst the game mode and the game state

42
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.879
are destroyed and respawned every time we transition level,

43
00:01:51.879 --> 00:01:54.920
obviously we would want our score to reset to zero, right?

44
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.280
And when we're going from one level to a different level,

45
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.400
maybe we've gone from the shooter level

46
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.439
to the car driving game mode.

47
00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:05.400
So we have different default pawns and things like that.

48
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.920
Whereas we want that stuff to kind of get,

49
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.120
we intentionally want it to be torn down and rebuilt,

50
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.840
the game instance is where we're

51
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.080
going to store things that persist between those levels.

52
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.360
So say, for example, we start the game

53
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.759
and client one chooses to be on team red.

54
00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:25.280
We want some way to store data that is going

55
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.159
to persist between two levels, right?

56
00:02:27.159 --> 00:02:29.919
They should still be on team red when they go into level two.

57
00:02:29.919 --> 00:02:31.599
And therefore, that's something that we

58
00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:33.319
might do in the game instance.

59
00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.280
So just as a kind of visual demonstration,

60
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.319
this same thing happening.

61
00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:40.159
We have a kind of imagining a multiplayer game set up here.

62
00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.639
We have level one.

63
00:02:41.639 --> 00:02:43.919
The game mode, again, only on our server

64
00:02:43.919 --> 00:02:45.639
because it's authoritative.

65
00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:47.919
The rules of the game are defined there.

66
00:02:47.919 --> 00:02:50.340
And the clients don't need to worry too much about them.

67
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.939
The game state exists on the server

68
00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:55.699
and is replicated to our clients.

69
00:02:55.699 --> 00:02:57.939
But both our client and our server

70
00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:00.139
have their own instances.

71
00:03:00.139 --> 00:03:02.819
And as we go from level one to level two,

72
00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:05.180
the server game instance remains

73
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.340
and the client instance remains.

74
00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.020
They don't communicate with each other,

75
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.610
but they are persistent between levels.

76
00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.650
And again, here you can see that when we start level two,

77
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.530
we get a second game mode and we get a second game

78
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.710
state because the game mode, the game state

79
00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.830
may have changed.

80
00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.310
Talking a little bit more about how we actually specify

81
00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.949
that game mode, there's a couple of different ways

82
00:03:27.949 --> 00:03:28.789
we could do this.

83
00:03:28.789 --> 00:03:31.750
We can go into the default engine any in our configs

84
00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.189
and specify our game mode here.

85
00:03:33.189 --> 00:03:36.069
So when you want to set the kind of default game

86
00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:38.469
mode for your game, you can do that here

87
00:03:38.469 --> 00:03:39.789
through the any files.

88
00:03:39.789 --> 00:03:41.949
Most of the time when you have an any file

89
00:03:41.949 --> 00:03:43.409
and it's got fields you can edit,

90
00:03:43.409 --> 00:03:46.710
you can edit it in a kind of more visually easy

91
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.069
to pass kind of way inside the engine

92
00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:50.189
through the project settings.

93
00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:52.469
So in the project settings maps and mode section,

94
00:03:52.469 --> 00:03:54.430
there's a default game mode there.

95
00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.270
But the other thing you can do is specify a different game

96
00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.150
mode per level.

97
00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.030
Like we said, maybe in one level

98
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.189
you're running around in first person shooter mode

99
00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:04.229
and maybe for another level you're driving cars.

100
00:04:04.229 --> 00:04:06.830
So that's something you can actually override in the world

101
00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.199
settings of each level.

102
00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:12.240
Beyond worrying about just the kind of more static data

103
00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.039
present in game mode, there is a bit of functionality

104
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:17.000
that is worth being concerned about.

105
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.680
The other thing to mention is that when

106
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.279
we're making a game mode, when we're

107
00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:23.199
making a class that inherits from game mode,

108
00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:25.839
there's actually two points we can branch from.

109
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:28.879
There's the game mode base class, which as the name kind

110
00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:32.439
of would imply is the base class for game mode.

111
00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:35.279
And then there is a class called game mode, which

112
00:04:35.279 --> 00:04:37.040
inherits from game mode base.

113
00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.899
Game mode essentially implements some additional multiplayer

114
00:04:40.899 --> 00:04:43.759
specific functionality on top of game mode base.

115
00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:46.279
So if you're making a single player standalone game,

116
00:04:46.279 --> 00:04:48.399
it's quite likely you'll want your game mode

117
00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:52.120
classes to inherit from game mode base, not the game mode

118
00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.120
class.

119
00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.680
So there's a set of virtual functions

120
00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.040
available to both of these that are just

121
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.459
worth talking a little bit about.

122
00:04:57.459 --> 00:04:58.879
There is a lot more than these,

123
00:04:58.879 --> 00:05:01.319
but these are just some kind of really common ones

124
00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:02.800
that you would want to utilize.

125
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.319
In it game is called really, really early in the game

126
00:05:05.319 --> 00:05:07.579
flow, and it's kind of like our initial kickoff point.

127
00:05:07.579 --> 00:05:10.019
It's called before we even initialized components.

128
00:05:10.019 --> 00:05:11.800
And it's essentially a point where

129
00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.000
we initialize any just super early data

130
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.060
that we need to be a certain way before anything else begins.

131
00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.899
Then kind of from that point onwards,

132
00:05:19.899 --> 00:05:21.980
we're going to talk about things that are fired

133
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.699
specifically on a per player basis.

134
00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:26.899
So first we have pre-login.

135
00:05:26.899 --> 00:05:30.459
This is essentially responsible for possibly rejecting

136
00:05:30.459 --> 00:05:31.379
a player's connection.

137
00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:34.939
So this is before that player actually exists in that game.

138
00:05:34.939 --> 00:05:37.639
It's kind of worth mentioning that there's

139
00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:39.939
kind of a non-trivial amount of time,

140
00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:41.939
or possibly non-trivial amount of time,

141
00:05:41.939 --> 00:05:44.300
between the pre-login functionality

142
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.259
and the actual login function for that player,

143
00:05:47.259 --> 00:05:49.620
especially if that player has a lot of content they need

144
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:50.500
to download.

145
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:53.540
There might be quite a long time between those two things.

146
00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:56.420
Post-login is the point at which we have succeeded

147
00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:56.819
logging in.

148
00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:58.399
Nothing's gone wrong with our download process.

149
00:05:58.399 --> 00:05:59.779
Our player has been accepted.

150
00:05:59.779 --> 00:06:02.220
So this is the first point where we can actually say, yes,

151
00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.980
this player is in this game.

152
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:05.939
They'll have a player controller at this point.

153
00:06:05.939 --> 00:06:07.319
So this might be the point where

154
00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:10.500
you display some kind of UI to say, hey,

155
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.860
this person has joined the game.

156
00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.779
Next, we go on to handling starting a new player.

157
00:06:15.779 --> 00:06:18.060
So once that player has started the game,

158
00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.579
we need to go through this process of injecting them

159
00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:21.779
into the actual game mode.

160
00:06:21.779 --> 00:06:25.100
And that's where handleStartNewPlayer comes in.

161
00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.579
At this point, you might be doing something like assigning

162
00:06:27.579 --> 00:06:30.500
them a team to be on.

163
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.100
And there might be certain data that extrapolates from that.

164
00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.620
And then handleStartingPlayer would then

165
00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.600
call eventually restartPlayer.

166
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.819
But obviously, as the name implies,

167
00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:42.339
restartPlayer is also called several times

168
00:06:42.339 --> 00:06:43.779
if your character dies.

169
00:06:43.779 --> 00:06:47.339
So restartPlayer gets called in with your player controller.

170
00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:49.600
And it's the response of the ability of restartPlayer

171
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:50.899
to perform a bunch of functionality

172
00:06:50.899 --> 00:06:52.500
about choosing a pawn for that player

173
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:53.939
and spawning it in that place.

174
00:06:53.939 --> 00:06:58.500
That will eventually then call spawnDefaultPawn at transform.

175
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.620
By default, that's just going to default back

176
00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.500
to what the game mode specifies as a default pawn.

177
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.220
But this is passed in with the player controller.

178
00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:10.019
So if for some reason, say for example,

179
00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.300
that player chose a character before they entered this level,

180
00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.220
you could specify specifically for this player controller

181
00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.379
spawning this character rather than the game mode's

182
00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:20.740
default pawn.

183
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.379
And then after all of that, those last two functions

184
00:07:24.379 --> 00:07:26.180
are going to be called multiple times

185
00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.540
as that player possibly dies and gets respawned.

186
00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.500
But finally, when that player chooses to leave,

187
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.220
when they either leave or the player is destroyed,

188
00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.220
onLogout is called.

189
00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:35.300
And this might be the point at which

190
00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.879
you want to display to other players

191
00:07:36.879 --> 00:07:38.779
that player has left the game.

192
00:07:38.779 --> 00:07:40.819
Then that's kind of shared functionality

193
00:07:40.819 --> 00:07:42.139
between the two of them.

194
00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:43.980
Game mode specifically, like we said,

195
00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.139
it's kind of more specifically geared to multiplayer games.

196
00:07:47.139 --> 00:07:49.579
Hypothetically, any of those things might be useful

197
00:07:49.579 --> 00:07:50.740
even in a single player game,

198
00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.939
because your player might join that map for the first time

199
00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:57.459
and you still want to edit some of this functionality.

200
00:07:57.459 --> 00:07:58.740
Game mode specifically,

201
00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.819
the kind of more multiplayer centric one,

202
00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:03.180
has access to this concept of match states.

203
00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.860
And you'll see that game mode itself

204
00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:06.100
overrides some of these functions.

205
00:08:06.139 --> 00:08:09.779
So say, for example, handleStartingNewPlayer,

206
00:08:09.779 --> 00:08:13.339
where game mode essentially just asks a couple of questions

207
00:08:13.339 --> 00:08:14.860
about whether or not we should be spawning

208
00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.100
that player's pawn in.

209
00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.319
One of the things game mode will check against

210
00:08:18.319 --> 00:08:20.259
is whether or not we're waiting to start.

211
00:08:20.259 --> 00:08:22.339
Waiting to start might be an example

212
00:08:22.339 --> 00:08:25.459
of our classic waiting for more players screen.

213
00:08:25.459 --> 00:08:27.139
So the game is technically starting,

214
00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:28.220
but there's just not enough players

215
00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:29.740
to start this game running.

216
00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:32.419
So game mode has an additional check

217
00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:34.200
for are we in waiting to start?

218
00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.000
If so, then don't spawn like their default pawn,

219
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.509
maybe spawn a spectator spawn or something like that.

220
00:08:40.509 --> 00:08:41.350
As we mentioned,

221
00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.029
there's a couple of different states available to us.

222
00:08:43.029 --> 00:08:45.190
We're entering the map, meaning things are starting up.

223
00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:47.669
Waiting to start, so when we're waiting for other players.

224
00:08:47.669 --> 00:08:50.590
In progress, as you expected, the game is ongoing.

225
00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:52.990
Waiting post match is people might be reviewing

226
00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.149
their scores, might be voting on the next map to go to,

227
00:08:56.149 --> 00:08:57.990
and leaving map is when we're transitioning

228
00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.450
into the next level.

229
00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.649
Game state is, as we already mentioned a little bit,

230
00:09:03.649 --> 00:09:07.529
is really for our game's runtime, real-time data.

231
00:09:07.529 --> 00:09:10.090
So it doesn't have so much in the way of functionality.

232
00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:11.210
It's not such a heavy class

233
00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.269
in terms of things we would need to override.

234
00:09:13.269 --> 00:09:15.610
It's really just a place we should feel quite comfortable

235
00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.090
to put any data that we want to be,

236
00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.570
that is broadly game-wide,

237
00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:23.090
that we want to be communicated to each of the players.

238
00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:24.970
It's worth mentioning though, specifically,

239
00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:27.330
that this is not on a per player basis.

240
00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:29.470
So this is the kind of global data

241
00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:31.090
for all the players to see.

242
00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:34.009
If we're worrying about data on a per player basis,

243
00:09:34.009 --> 00:09:36.769
that's handled somewhere else, which is the player state,

244
00:09:36.769 --> 00:09:39.190
which we'll get onto next.

245
00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:42.509
So we mentioned very briefly about player-specific data.

246
00:09:42.509 --> 00:09:45.870
This isn't one of those classes that we mentioned before,

247
00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:48.110
but just because we're talking about replicating data

248
00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:50.049
across the server, it's worth touching briefly

249
00:09:50.049 --> 00:09:51.429
on the player state.

250
00:09:51.429 --> 00:09:54.710
Much like the game state, the player state is intended

251
00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:58.429
for holding the, hence the name, state of our player.

252
00:09:58.429 --> 00:10:01.269
If we kind of look at an example setup here,

253
00:10:01.269 --> 00:10:04.250
we've got the server for our game.

254
00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:08.049
That server would have a player controller for player one

255
00:10:08.049 --> 00:10:09.909
and a player controller for player two.

256
00:10:09.909 --> 00:10:12.629
It would also have state for each of those players.

257
00:10:12.629 --> 00:10:16.789
Now, whilst the server kind of contains all of this stuff,

258
00:10:16.789 --> 00:10:18.190
our clients would actually see

259
00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:19.669
a slightly restricted view,

260
00:10:19.669 --> 00:10:23.590
where each client sees their own player controller.

261
00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.590
They don't see every other player's player controller.

262
00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.669
That's what player state is for.

263
00:10:28.669 --> 00:10:33.269
So a little bit like game mode to game state,

264
00:10:33.269 --> 00:10:37.070
player state is the same thing in reflection to,

265
00:10:37.070 --> 00:11:02.740
oh my God, I've lost the flow there.

266
00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:06.500
So whereas the game state is for the game-wide state

267
00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:09.820
that is gonna be shared information across all players,

268
00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.159
for player-specific information,

269
00:11:12.159 --> 00:11:14.220
that's where it would come to the player state.

270
00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:18.440
If we kind of just take an example kind of setup here,

271
00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.259
we have the server.

272
00:11:20.299 --> 00:11:22.220
For each client connected to the server,

273
00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.299
the server is gonna spawn in a player controller

274
00:11:24.299 --> 00:11:25.940
and a player state.

275
00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.379
If we look at each of these clients, however,

276
00:11:28.379 --> 00:11:31.240
a client sees their own player controller,

277
00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.940
but they don't see other client's player controller.

278
00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.460
So client one doesn't see client two's player controller,

279
00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:38.720
client two doesn't see player one's player controller.

280
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:41.779
So for any information that is shared between the players,

281
00:11:41.779 --> 00:11:43.559
if I as a player need to see information

282
00:11:43.559 --> 00:11:44.860
about another player,

283
00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.899
that's something that should be put on the player state.

284
00:11:46.899 --> 00:11:50.980
Every client will have all of the other client's

285
00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:54.179
replicated information done through the player state.

286
00:11:54.179 --> 00:11:56.220
That might, very much like the player controller,

287
00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:58.299
this information persists beyond pawns.

288
00:11:58.299 --> 00:11:59.860
So if you think about it,

289
00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:01.820
whereas the pawn might have something like health,

290
00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.980
something like that player's score

291
00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:07.740
or the last location that player spawned at

292
00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.820
or their combo bonus or something like that,

293
00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:13.179
any information that is kind of like abstracted

294
00:12:13.179 --> 00:12:14.940
from the pawn specifically,

295
00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:16.759
but other clients would need to see

296
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:19.399
that's going to be on your player state.

297
00:12:19.399 --> 00:12:20.980
Obviously it's replicated,

298
00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.600
so therefore it supports all your typical RPCs.

299
00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:25.399
And very much like the game state,

300
00:12:25.399 --> 00:12:28.039
your player state is specified by your game mode.

301
00:12:28.039 --> 00:12:29.279
So if we can just see,

302
00:12:29.279 --> 00:12:31.879
you can see you specify in your game mode,

303
00:12:31.879 --> 00:12:34.320
which player state class to use for this level

304
00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.460
or for your game in general.

305
00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:37.700
So in our last presentation,

306
00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.299
we talked a little bit about the flow

307
00:12:39.299 --> 00:12:41.279
that an actor goes through as it gets spawned in.

308
00:12:41.279 --> 00:12:42.899
And we saw that that's different

309
00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:44.460
depending on if you're playing an editor,

310
00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:46.480
if that actor has been spawned in

311
00:12:46.799 --> 00:12:48.039
when the level was created

312
00:12:48.039 --> 00:12:49.799
or when you spawned it in at runtime,

313
00:12:49.799 --> 00:12:51.519
it's somewhat similar for the engine.

314
00:12:51.519 --> 00:12:53.320
And that's because obviously there's two ways

315
00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.240
we can come into the engine.

316
00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.179
We can come into it through the editor

317
00:12:56.179 --> 00:12:58.159
and we can come in through a standalone game

318
00:12:58.159 --> 00:12:59.919
that we've packaged.

319
00:12:59.919 --> 00:13:02.559
For the most part, these are relatively similar,

320
00:13:02.559 --> 00:13:05.240
but there's a few key things to kind of bear in mind.

321
00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.559
Firstly, when we're in editor,

322
00:13:07.559 --> 00:13:09.139
our engine has started at the point

323
00:13:09.139 --> 00:13:10.039
we started our editor.

324
00:13:10.039 --> 00:13:12.559
So there's this kind of period of time

325
00:13:12.559 --> 00:13:14.919
before we hit our play and editor button

326
00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:19.320
where the engine will kind of exist,

327
00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:21.679
but we don't yet have a game instance.

328
00:13:21.679 --> 00:13:25.139
On the other side, when we've made a packaged game,

329
00:13:25.139 --> 00:13:26.279
game instance actually exists

330
00:13:26.279 --> 00:13:29.000
before our engine gets started.

331
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.759
That's kind of a key discrepancy.

332
00:13:30.759 --> 00:13:33.360
There's also some slightly different functions are called.

333
00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:34.840
We have initialized PIE

334
00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:36.340
when we're calling the play and editor,

335
00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.000
whereas when we're doing a packaged game,

336
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:42.279
we get game instance initialized standalone.

337
00:13:42.279 --> 00:13:44.399
But ultimately it all funnels together

338
00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:46.080
into this word begin play.

339
00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.820
Again, just worth explaining here

340
00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.879
that this kind of really highlights

341
00:13:49.879 --> 00:13:52.559
the fundamental nature of the game instance,

342
00:13:52.559 --> 00:13:53.759
that this is really created

343
00:13:53.759 --> 00:13:55.299
right at the beginning of the engine,

344
00:13:55.299 --> 00:13:57.960
right when our XE first starts up.

345
00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:00.879
So before anything to do with a world exists as a concept,

346
00:14:00.879 --> 00:14:03.580
we already have our game instance.

347
00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.659
Next, once we're actually within a world,

348
00:14:05.659 --> 00:14:07.419
that's where our world itself begins play.

349
00:14:07.419 --> 00:14:08.779
And then one of the first things we'll do

350
00:14:08.779 --> 00:14:09.919
is create our game mode.

351
00:14:09.919 --> 00:14:11.879
And from there on, we have the game mode flow.

352
00:14:11.879 --> 00:14:13.379
So our game mode start play

353
00:14:13.419 --> 00:14:15.419
and then our game mode start match.

354
00:14:15.419 --> 00:14:17.100
It's only after that,

355
00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:19.460
that we begin to spawn other actors and begin the game.

356
00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:23.419
So this really can demonstrate the primacy of the game mode

357
00:14:23.419 --> 00:14:25.580
as a really fundamental gameplay class.

358
00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:26.860
You can trust that the game mode

359
00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:29.019
is gonna have its start behavior, its start logic,

360
00:14:29.019 --> 00:14:30.779
and therefore its begin play

361
00:14:30.779 --> 00:14:32.139
called before any of the classes.

362
00:14:32.139 --> 00:14:33.879
So even at a purely blueprint level

363
00:14:33.879 --> 00:14:36.580
where you might not be getting into really fundamentals

364
00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:38.200
of all the engine flow,

365
00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:39.740
you can kind of trust that

366
00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.460
if you want to inject some logic

367
00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.539
that you can trust is going to be called

368
00:14:43.539 --> 00:14:44.720
before player controllers,

369
00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:47.340
before pawns get spawned or anything like this,

370
00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:48.580
put that within the game mode,

371
00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:49.980
because that's gonna be one of the first things

372
00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:53.080
that come within your runtime level.
